# Short-Term Memory. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([2], "What is memory?", "Memory includes retaining, retrieving and using information about stimuli, images, events, ideas and skills after the original information is no longer present. A past experience can influence current or future behavior without being visible in the environment."),
 ([2], "How did Endel Tulving describe episodic memory?", "Endel Tulving described episodic memory as a kind of mental time travel, because remembering an event connects it back to the experienced past."),
 ([3], "What are encoding, storage and retrieval?", "Encoding converts information into a usable form for storage, storage retains it, and retrieval makes it available again."),
 ([3], "Is memory an unchanged copy of an experience?", "No. Memory is an active system that can organize and alter information rather than retaining an unchanged copy in every case."),
 ([3], "Why does a failure to use information not tell you which memory operation went wrong?", "A failure to use information could reflect a problem at more than one of the operations, encoding, storage or retrieval, so the failure alone does not identify which one failed."),
 ([4], "What kinds of memory does the memory tree distinguish?", "The memory tree distinguishes sensory memory, short-term memory and long-term memory."),
 ([4], "What are declarative memory and procedural memory?", "Long-term memory includes declarative memory, which contains episodic events and semantic knowledge, and procedural memory, which concerns skills."),
 ([4], "Where do episodic and semantic memory sit in the memory tree?", "They are branches of declarative memory, which is part of long-term memory. They are not additional short-term stores."),
 ([5], "What are the three stores of Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) modal model?", "Sensory memory, short-term memory and long-term memory."),
 ([5], "How long does each store in the modal model hold information, and how much?", "Sensory memory briefly retains incoming stimulation for seconds or fractions of a second. Short-term memory holds five to seven items for about 15 to 20 seconds. Long-term memory retains large amounts for years or decades."),
 ([5], "What is rehearsal in the modal model?", "Rehearsal is a control process that can keep information available and support transfer."),
 ([6], "What happens to the information when you look at a phone directory?", "The many pieces of visual information enter sensory memory first, and the store briefly preserves them so attention can select relevant material. Not every detail is retained indefinitely."),
 ([6], "Are sensory registration and durable learning the same outcome?", "No. They are different outcomes, because something can be registered briefly in sensory memory without being learned."),
 ([7], "What does attention do between sensory memory and short-term memory?", "Focusing on a particular phone number transfers that information into short-term memory. Selection limits which part of the large sensory input receives further processing."),
 ([8], "What is maintenance rehearsal, and what does it do for a phone number?", "Maintenance rehearsal repeats the information, so repeating the number keeps it available in short-term memory long enough to make a call. It prolongs access that would otherwise be vulnerable to loss or interference."),
 ([9], "How can a rehearsed phone number end up in long-term memory?", "A rehearsed number can become represented in long-term memory, so later use no longer depends on the directory still being visible."),
 ([10], "What happens when a stored number is later retrieved?", "It is retrieved from long-term memory into short-term memory for dialing. Remembering requires bringing stored information back into an active form, so the movement is not only one way toward long-term storage."),
 ([11], "What are an icon and an echo?", "An icon is a fleeting visual representation, and an echo is a brief continuation of auditory information after a sound. The two differ in modality, and neither is a durable representation used to remember something much later."),
 ([12], "What is persistence of vision?", "Persistence of vision is the brief retention of the perception of light, which contributes to a sparkler’s trail and to the continuity experienced from film frames."),
 ([12], "Does the large capacity of sensory memory mean it lasts a long time?", "No. Sensory memory holds much information for a short interval, but its large capacity does not imply long duration, because the information decays quickly."),
 ([13], "What did Sperling (1960) do, and what question did it address?", "Sperling (1960) flashed an array of letters and asked participants to report what they had seen. The question was whether limited report reflects limited initial registration or rapid loss before all letters could be reported."),
 ([14], "What are whole report, partial report and delayed partial report in Sperling (1960)?", "In Sperling (1960), whole report had participants report as many letters as possible, partial report used a tone after the display to identify which row to recall, and in delayed partial report the tone occurred after an additional delay."),
 ([14], "Why does strong immediate partial report in Sperling (1960) show that several rows were available at first?", "In Sperling (1960), the row is not identified until after viewing, so accurate report of whichever row is cued implies that information about multiple rows was initially available."),
 ([15], "What was in Sperling’s (1960) array, and what did the cue do?", "The array was X M L T, A F N B and C D Z P. A cue after the array selects a row for report rather than telling the observer in advance which row to memorize, and increasing cue delay lets the sensory trace fade."),
 ([15], "What does Sperling’s (1960) procedure separate?", "It separates initial availability from the ability to report every item before it disappears."),
 ([16], "What were Sperling’s (1960) results for whole, immediate partial and delayed partial report?", "Whole report averaged 4.5 of 12 letters, 37.5 percent. Immediate partial report averaged 3.3 of 4 letters, about 82 percent, whichever row was selected. Delayed partial report averaged 1 of 4, or 25 percent."),
 ([17], "Does the whole-report limit of four to five letters in Sperling (1960) show that only that many were perceived?", "No. In Sperling (1960), immediate partial report permits accurate report of whichever row is cued, so report limitations can conceal greater initial availability."),
 ([17], "What do Sperling’s (1960) results say about the capacity and duration of sensory memory?", "Sensory memory has large capacity but rapid decay, with most information lost within about one second unless it is selected for further processing."),
 ([18], "What happens to partial-report accuracy as the cue is delayed in Sperling (1960)?", "In Sperling (1960), it decreases and approaches whole-report performance. The display is unchanged and only the interval before selection varies, which links the partial-report advantage to a rapidly fading representation. It does not support indefinite retention of the whole array."),
 ([19], "What is short-term memory, and what is memory span?", "Short-term memory retains small amounts briefly and is sensitive to interruption and interference. Memory span is the amount that can be recalled accurately in a task."),
 ([19], "What is the traditional estimate of memory span?", "Seven plus or minus two units. It depends on the kind of units and the task, and should not be used for every visual-memory capacity result."),
 ([20], "What is recoding?", "Recoding reorganizes or modifies information in short-term memory."),
 ([20], "How do maintenance rehearsal and elaborative rehearsal differ?", "Maintenance rehearsal repeats information to prolong its availability. Elaborative rehearsal links new information with existing long-term knowledge, which supports longer-term learning."),
 ([21], "What is short-term memory’s role in current thought?", "It includes selected sensory input and information retrieved from long-term memory, and it is needed to understand sentences, do arithmetic, dial a number and navigate. It acts as a window on the present."),
 ([22], "What does the case of Clive Wearing show?", "Clive Wearing is a case of memory impairment. Keeping immediate information and remembering across events are different memory functions, so a case like his has to be analyzed function by function."),
 ([23], "How do duration and capacity differ in memory?", "Duration is how long information remains available, and capacity is how much can be held."),
 ([23], "How do rehearsal and chunking affect the measurement of memory stores?", "Rehearsal can extend duration and chunking can extend effective capacity, so a result can reflect the strategy used as well as the memory system being studied."),
 ([24], "What was the Brown–Peterson procedure of Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "Participants were shown three letters followed by a number, counted backward by threes during a delay of 3 to 18 seconds, and then recalled the letters."),
 ([24], "Why did Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) have participants count backward by threes?", "Counting prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal, so comparing delays measures retention when participants cannot simply keep repeating the letters."),
 ([25], "What happened to recall on the first trial in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "Recall was high at three seconds and stayed relatively high at eighteen seconds, because no earlier trigrams from the task competed with the current one."),
 ([26], "What happened to recall on the third trial in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "Recall at eighteen seconds was lower than on the first trial, because earlier trigrams now provided competing information. The same delay can have different effects depending on the preceding trials."),
 ([27], "What happened to recall after many trials in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "Recall at longer delays was substantially reduced, because accumulated earlier material can interfere with the currently tested trigram."),
 ([28], "Why does the average over all trials give a misleading impression in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "The average shows a decline from shorter to longer delays, but it combines early trials with little interference and later trials with more. It can hide important differences by trial position, so the overall curve can hide important differences by trial position."),
 ([29], "How do delay and trial history combine in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "Percent recalled decreases with longer delays, but the pattern interacts with where a trial occurs in the sequence. Long-delay performance worsens as more trials are completed, so delay and accumulated interference jointly affect retention."),
 ([30], "What are decay and proactive interference?", "Decay is loss associated with the passage of time. Proactive interference occurs when earlier information disrupts remembering newer information."),
 ([30], "What is the effective duration of short-term memory, and what affects it?", "About 15 to 20 seconds, with rehearsal and interference conditions affecting the observed outcome."),
 ([31], "What is interference, and how do the two kinds differ?", "Interference occurs when memories compete with or replace one another, so forgetting is not explained solely by elapsed time. Retroactive and proactive interference differ in the direction of the competition."),
 ([32], "What is retroactive interference?", "Retroactive interference occurs when new information disrupts memory for older information. Learning a new phone number making the old one harder to retrieve is an example."),
 ([33], "What is proactive interference?", "Proactive interference occurs when old information disrupts memory for newer information. Previous parking locations interfering with remembering where you parked today is an example."),
 ([33], "What can reduce both kinds of interference?", "Both can be reduced when events are distinctive."),
 ([34], "What is digit span?", "Digit span measures the longest sequence of digits recalled correctly in order. Capacity differs between individuals and can be affected by strategies."),
 ([35], "How is digit span scored?", "The longest row reproduced completely and in order defines the span. One successful short list does not establish the limit, and one missed digit makes a longer list incorrect."),
 ([36], "What did Miller (1956) propose?", "Miller (1956), in The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two, proposed an influential capacity description in which the units remembered can differ across tasks."),
 ([37], "Does Miller’s (1956) number mean every short-term system stores exactly seven raw items?", "No. The units can be digits, words, numbers or phrases, and a unit can contain different amounts of information."),
 ([37], "What did Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) compare?", "They compared colored squares with more complex objects using change detection. The emphasis is on the amount or complexity of information, not a fixed number of equally sized items."),
 ([38], "What is visual short-term memory, and how is it measured?", "Visual short-term memory, VSTM, is measured by comparing displays after a delay. Its capacity can be smaller than span for digit lists or unrelated words."),
 ([39], "What happens in the first display, S1, of a change-detection task?", "Participants view an initial array briefly, 100 milliseconds, and must retain relevant visual properties after it disappears."),
 ([40], "What must a short-term visual representation preserve?", "The features needed for comparison. Color, shape, orientation and complexity can affect how demanding that retention is."),
 ([41], "What happens in the second display, S2, of a change-detection task?", "S2 appears for 2,000 milliseconds, and participants judge whether it is the same or different. Correct performance requires comparing S2 with the retained S1."),
 ([42], "What does set size do to change-detection accuracy?", "Accuracy falls sharply when set size exceeds roughly four items, giving a capacity of about three to four items in visual working memory."),
 ([43], "How does stimulus complexity affect visual capacity?", "Colored squares support a higher estimated capacity than more complex polygons or shaded cubes. Treating all stimuli as equally sized items hides these differences."),
 ([44], "Why is a capacity estimate for one stimulus category not universal?", "Familiarity and the structure attributed to a stimulus can affect what counts as a unit of information, so differences must be interpreted relative to the stimulus classes used. Chinese-character-like forms were also compared."),
 ([45], "What is chunking, and what did Ericsson and colleagues (1980) show?", "Chunking combines smaller units into larger meaningful units. Ericsson and colleagues (1980) trained a student to reach a digit span of 79 by using familiar running times to organize sequences."),
 ([45], "What did Chase and Simon (1973) find?", "Chess expertise supports grouping meaningful positions. The gain depends on knowledge-based organization and not on expanding every raw memory limit equally."),
 ([46], "How do chess masters differ from beginners in recalling piece placements in Chase and Simon (1973)?", "In Chase and Simon (1973), masters recall more correct placements for meaningful game arrangements, but the advantage is greatly reduced for random arrangements."),
 ([47], "What are control processes, and what does the memory palace show?", "Control processes include rehearsal, strategies for making material memorable and directing attention. The memory palace organizes information through a remembered spatial structure. These active operations lead toward working memory, which concerns using and managing currently available information."),
]
R = [
 ("How do attention and rehearsal connect the three stores of the modal model?", "Attention selects part of the large sensory input for short-term memory, and rehearsal keeps that information available and can support its transfer to long-term memory. Retrieval then brings long-term information back into short-term memory.", [5,7,8,9,10]),
 ("How does sensory memory differ from short-term memory in capacity and duration?", "Sensory memory has large capacity but decays within about one second unless selected, while short-term memory holds only a small amount, five to seven items, for about 15 to 20 seconds.", [5,17,19]),
 ("Why does Sperling’s (1960) result show that the whole-report limit is a report limit?", "Immediate partial report was about 82 percent for whichever row was cued, so far more was available than the four to five letters reported in whole report. The limit came from rapid decay before everything could be reported.", [14,16,17]),
 ("How are Sperling’s (1960) delay manipulation and the Brown–Peterson delay similar, and what do they test?", "Both vary the time before report. Sperling’s delayed cue shows a fading sensory trace, while Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) vary the delay in short-term memory while blocking rehearsal.", [18,24]),
 ("How do decay and interference differ as explanations of forgetting?", "Decay is loss from the passage of time, while interference is memories competing or replacing one another. The Brown–Peterson trial pattern is evidence that forgetting is not explained solely by elapsed time.", [29,30,31]),
 ("Which kind of interference does the Brown–Peterson pattern show, and how does it differ from retroactive interference?", "It shows proactive interference, where earlier trigrams disrupt the new one. Retroactive interference runs the other way, with new information disrupting older memories.", [27,30,32,33]),
 ("How do maintenance rehearsal and elaborative rehearsal differ in what they accomplish?", "Maintenance rehearsal prolongs availability in short-term memory, while elaborative rehearsal links information with long-term knowledge and supports longer-term learning.", [8,20]),
 ("How do duration, capacity and strategy interact when stores are measured?", "Rehearsal can extend duration and chunking can extend effective capacity, so a measurement can reflect the strategy as well as the store.", [23,45]),
 ("Why is there no single capacity number for short-term memory?", "Estimates include five to seven items, seven plus or minus two units, and about three to four items for visual short-term memory. The estimate depends on the kind of units, the task and the stimulus complexity.", [5,19,36,42,43]),
 ("How do digit span and change detection measure capacity differently?", "Digit span measures the longest list recalled correctly in order, while change detection compares a second display with a retained first display after a delay.", [34,35,39,41]),
 ("How do Miller (1956) and Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) differ on what capacity is counted in?", "Miller (1956) counts units such as digits or words, while Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) emphasize the amount or complexity of information in each item.", [36,37,43]),
 ("How does chunking explain the chess results of Chase and Simon (1973)?", "Masters group pieces into meaningful chunks of familiar relationships. Random arrangements remove those relationships, so the advantage mostly disappears.", [45,46]),
 ("How do Ericsson and colleagues (1980) and Chase and Simon (1973) show the same principle?", "Both show that knowledge-based organization extends effective capacity. The student used familiar running times to organize digits, and chess masters used familiar piece relationships, without expanding every raw memory limit equally.", [45,46]),
 ("How do encoding, storage and retrieval relate to the three stores?", "Encoding, storage and retrieval are operations on information, while sensory, short-term and long-term memory are the stores. Information is encoded for storage, retained in a store, and retrieved back into an active form, as when a phone number returns from long-term to short-term memory.", [3,5,10]),
 ("How do the memory tree and the modal model each organize memory, and where do they overlap?", "The memory tree distinguishes sensory, short-term and long-term memory, with long-term memory including declarative and procedural memory. The modal model distinguishes the same three stores and adds rehearsal as a control process. Both treat sensory, short-term and long-term memory as distinct.", [4,5]),
 ("How is the icon related to persistence of vision and sensory memory?", "An icon is a fleeting visual representation in sensory memory, and persistence of vision is the brief retention of the perception of light that contributes to a sparkler’s trail and to the continuity experienced from film frames. Both are brief and neither is a durable representation.", [11,12]),
 ("Why must different memory functions be distinguished when studying a case like Clive Wearing?", "Keeping immediate information and remembering across events are different functions, so analyzing the case function by function avoids assuming one memory system explains everything.", [21,22,23]),
 ("How does short-term memory as a window on the present depend on long-term memory?", "Short-term memory includes information retrieved from long-term memory as well as selected sensory input, so current thought draws on both.", [10,21]),
 ("How do control processes lead into working memory?", "Rehearsal, memorability strategies and directing attention are active operations on available information, which is what working memory concerns, using and managing information that is currently available.", [20,47]),
 ("How do the Brown–Peterson counting task and chunking each relate to strategy in memory measurement?", "Counting backward by threes prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal, so retention is measured without simple repetition. Chunking extends effective capacity, so results must account for control processes such as rehearsal and chunking.", [23,24,45]),
 ("How do the tasks of Sperling (1960) and Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) each show that a capacity estimate depends on how it is measured?", "In Sperling (1960), the whole-report limit hid the larger initial availability, and in change detection the capacity estimate depends on stimulus type, delay and the response required.", [17,37,42,43]),
]
